WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.238
Why this matters.

2
00:00:01.238 --> 00:00:03.769
For twenty years Group
Policy did a great deal

3
00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:06.300
of governance without
anyone writing it down.

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.965
A computer sat in an organizational
unit (OU), GPOs linked to that OU

5
00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:15.975
and its parents applied in a fixed
order, and the closest container won.

6
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:19.918
That one structure was the targeting
model, the precedence model,

7
00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:23.787
the delegation model, a timing
guarantee and an evidence source.

8
00:00:23.787 --> 00:00:28.832
Microsoft's planning guidance says it
plainly: Group Policy enforcement isn't

9
00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:33.876
possible on cloud-native endpoints, which
are joined to Entra ID, not to a domain.

10
00:00:33.876 --> 00:00:38.050
Intune can deliver most of the
settings, but each mechanism changes.

11
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.963
Assignment to groups is flat.

12
00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:43.100
Assignment filters evaluate
device properties at

13
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.237
enrollment and check-in, up
to two hundred per tenant.

14
00:00:46.237 --> 00:00:50.256
A platform script runs once and
times out after thirty minutes;

15
00:00:50.256 --> 00:00:53.812
Remediations rerun every
twenty-four hours by default.

16
00:00:53.812 --> 00:00:56.450
There's no built-in
answer for mapped drives.

17
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.612
Devices check in about every eight
hours instead of every ninety minutes.

18
00:01:00.612 --> 00:01:04.550
And per-setting status is reported
after check-in, so it can lag.

19
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.387
The conflict rules change too.

20
00:01:06.387 --> 00:01:10.490
When two Intune configuration
policies set one setting differently,

21
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:14.026
the conflict is reported and the
setting may not apply at all.

22
00:01:14.026 --> 00:01:17.112
Compliance policies take
the most restrictive value.

23
00:01:17.112 --> 00:01:21.720
And on a hybrid device that gets
both a GPO and an MDM policy,

24
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.024
Group Policy wins by default.

25
00:01:24.024 --> 00:01:29.259
MDM wins over GP reverses that only
for the Policy CSP; for other CSPs,

26
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:34.587
such as Defender, Microsoft warns of
a race with no guaranteed winner.

27
00:01:34.587 --> 00:01:37.663
So the program's rule is
regenerate, not copy.

28
00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:43.375
Microsoft's own guidance is not to lift
and shift: identify the mandatory settings,

29
00:01:43.375 --> 00:01:47.040
compare them with MDM, and
treat the gaps as blockers.
